A1. The first clothes tree. ~ FIG
A4. This should be kept up by anyone who wants to go far in the world. ~ AIRSHIP
A10. A buzzer. ~ BEE
A13. You'll rarely find Horizontal 10 this way. ~ IDLE
A15. The stars who want to shine in the films must keep in this. ~ FOCUS
A16. An evergreen tree of New Zealand. ~ TAWA
A17. A kick in the pants. ~ BOOST
A19. A passenger of a side door Pullman. ~ BUM
A20. A game of holding hands. ~ POKER
A21. A genus of the water-lily family. ~ LOTUS
A23. What some American girls buy in Europe. ~ MONIKER
A26. Dad's sparring partner. ~ MATER
A28. Even though this sometimes rises it never gets out of bed. ~ RIVER
A30. Where Horizontal 4 is always going. ~ UP
A32. This is poisonous. ~ TOXIC
A34. A heavyweight. ~ TON
A35. A state. (Abbr.) ~ VA
A36. This saves a lot of money ~ MISER
A38. This is always waiting on some one. ~ VALET
A40. An awkward situation. ~ FIX
A41. A sign used to denote addition. ~ PLUS
A42. A love token. ~ F.VOR
A43. The wise men of the East. ~ MAGI
A44. A large pile of earth. ~ KOP
A45. This is very painful to the younger generation. ~ COLIC
A46. This helped Houdini out of many a jam. ~ MAGIC
A47. What you must have to get in the movies. ~ IT
A48. This kind of girl is now obsolete. ~ COY
A49. This can't get along with—or without—capital. ~ LABOR
A51. The wettest State in the Union in 1927. (Abbr.) ~ LA
A52. These hold the wires in Warsaw. ~ POLES
A54. The correct thing to use in an argument. ~ LOGIC
A56. The great divide. ~ DIVORCE
A58. These are always running things down. ~ AUTOS
A60. This is always on a spree. ~ TOPER
A61. This gave an old hen a pain in the neck. ~ AXE
A63. This often faces powder. ~ LAPEL
A65. The kind of draft that puts you in a hole. ~ OVER
A66. This is a sticker. ~ BRIER
A68. A monk of Tibet. ~ LAMA
A69. By means of. ~ PER
A70. Insects. ~ BEETLES
A71. This goes over the top. ~ LID

D1. What G. W. told his father when he said he couldn't lie. ~ FIB
D2. The golden calf. ~ IDOL
D3. There's no fun in this. ~ GLOOM
D5. A word much used by prognosticators. ~ IF
D6. What people with taking ways are apt to do. ~ ROB
D7. This comes to the surface when things start to boil. ~ SCUM
D8. This is the best kind of fun. ~ HUMOR
D9. Part of the verb to be. ~ IS
D10. A loafer. ~ BAKER
D11. A vessel for holding water—or what have you? ~ EWER
D12. Where an African belle wears her wedding ring. ~ EAR
D14. These are found on both banks of the Hudson. ~ ESTATES
D16. A symbol of good faith. ~ TOKEN
D18. This is always at the head of the class. ~ TUTOR
D20. Things always go around on this. ~ PIVOT
D22. This is much in evidence on Broadway. ~ SEX
D24. Potassium nitrate. ~ NITER
D25. Cinderella's sport model coach. ~ PUMPKIN
D27. An antagonist. ~ RIV.L
D29. A place to hold petting parties if you want to get somewhere. ~ TAXICAB
D31. What the U. S. needs on the Ship of State. ~ PILOT
D33. To raise frivolous objection. ~ CAVIL
D35. It takes a wide-awake person to keep this. ~ VIGIL
D37. What King Arthur liked to do at the Round Table. ~ SUP
D39. The kind of little pig that stays at home. ~ LOCAL
D40. That tired feeling. ~ FAG
D42. The lobby. ~ FOYER
D43. The rights that married folks scrap for. ~ MARITAL
D45. A paint complexion. ~ COLOR
D46. A person of great wealth or influence. ~ MOGUL
D48. What most dry agents work under. ~ COVER
D50. A choker. ~ BOA
D52. This blows around Scotland. ~ PIPER
D53. What Al Smith gave the G. O. P. when he said "I choose to run." ~ SCARE
D55. A resin used in varnishes. ~ COPAL
D56. This informed Noah when the country went dry. ~ DOVE
D57. A way to get out of a troublesome situation. ~ EXIT
D59. This means half. ~ SEMI
D60. This can stand going around. ~ TOP
D62. A long tailed fish. ~ EEL
D64. A stripling. ~ LAD
D66. To exist. ~ BE
D67. In law, of the thing or matter. ~ RE


